Yes,
the genetic group is a permanent attribute for each individual. I was able to
get group density predictions using predict(), but there have been some odd
results described below.

#Null model results in
61 individuals (61 EN 61 RN), which is similar to other models I have run

fl <- secr.fit(mch,
CL = FALSE, model = list(D~1, g0~1, sigma~1), details = list(sigma = 920.836),
mask = extractedmask, detectfn = 'HHN')

region.N(ffl)

#Null model just
identifying that groups are present results in 15 individuals (15 EN 31 RN). This
is underestimating the number of individuals from what I’ve observed on the
landscape

flg <- secr.fit(fch,
CL = FALSE, groups = 'Genetics', model = list(D~1, g0~1, sigma~1), details =
list(sigma = 920.836), mask = extractedmask, detectfn = 'HHN')

region.N(fflg)

#Model placing genetics
as an individual covariate on lambda results in 40 individuals (40 EN 55 RN)

flgg <-
secr.fit(fch, CL = FALSE, groups = 'Genetics', model = list(D~1, g0~g,
sigma~1), details = list(sigma = 920.836), mask = extractedmask, detectfn =
'HHN')

region.N(fflgg)

#Trying a couple of different
ways to specify all levels get me the same results each time. The density
estimates multiplied by the hectares result in a prediction of 40 individuals
for each of the four genetic pools represented by this sex, which doesn’t sum
